---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'pause', label: 'The pause that costs more than its length' },
  { id: 'compare', label: 'The same test, two loops' },
  { id: 'numbers', label: 'What local calls measure' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'Feel the difference' },
];
const startCmd = `localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env)"`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="the-2ms-inner-loop"
  kind="Perspective"
  deck="The slow part of a cloud test isn't your code. It's the distance. Put the services on localhost and the wait between saving a file and knowing whether it works drops below the point where you notice it."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/performance/', label: 'See the benchmarks' }}
  {contents}
  next={{ posts: ['tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode', 'one-second-cloud-state-resets'], doc: { href: '/docs/performance/', label: 'Performance benchmarks: every service and CLI command' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="pause">The pause that costs more than its length</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">You change a line in a handler and run the integration test. It creates a bucket, gets a token, writes an object, publishes a message, polls for delivery and cleans up. Each of those is a round trip to a data centre, and together they take half a minute.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Half a minute is an awkward length. It is too long to stare at the terminal and too short to do anything else, so you glance at chat, and now the cost is no longer thirty seconds. It is the time it takes to remember what you were testing. Do that all afternoon and the day's work is mostly recovering your place.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Unit tests don't have this problem, which is why people write code against them so fluently. The difference isn't the kind of test. It's how far away the thing being tested is.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="compare">The same test, two loops</h2>
    <table class="blog-compare mt-5">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Remote project</th><th scope="col">LocalCloud on localhost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Each API call</th><td>A network round trip, with TLS and a token</td><td>A few milliseconds on loopback</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A ten-test suite</th><td>Long enough to switch windows</td><td>Done before you look up</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Leftover state</th><td>Shared with everyone, cleaned up by hand</td><td>Yours alone, reset in under a second</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">On a train or a flaky VPN</th><td>Blocked</td><td>Works offline</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost of running it again</th><td>Billed per request</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-4">Nothing about the test changes. Your application still uses the standard Google Cloud client libraries; the exported environment points them at <code>127.0.0.1</code> instead of the internet.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="numbers">What local calls measure</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">These are median latencies from the published <a href="/docs/performance/">benchmarks</a>, measured through the same clients your code uses:</p>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Memorystore:</strong> 1 ms for a GET or SET.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloud Storage:</strong> 2 ms to read an object, 5 ms to write one.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloud SQL and Secret Manager:</strong> 2 ms for a point read or write.</li>
      <li><strong>Pub/Sub:</strong> 3 ms to publish a message.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">At those speeds an integration test behaves like a unit test. You can run it on every save, which changes how you write the code: smaller steps, checked as you go, instead of a batch of changes and a hopeful run at the end.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Starting up doesn't break the rhythm either. The runtime is ready about five seconds after a cold start, and if it is already running, the command returns in a fraction of a second.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Feel the difference</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Install the CLI from the <a href="/docs/#install-the-cli">quick start</a>, then run two commands and your existing test suite in the same shell:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Start and point your SDKs at localhost" code={startCmd} copy="copy-blog-2ms-start" />
    <p class="mt-4">Run the suite twice. The first run tells you whether the tests pass. The second tells you how it feels when the answer comes back before you have had time to wonder.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
